# Everything needed to reach AWS, and nothing else.
#
# Copy to `infra/.env` and fill in. NEVER commit the real .env.
#
# Application configuration is NOT here. It lives in one AWS Secrets Manager
# secret (Infisical syncs it there), and the container's entrypoint loads it at
# startup. See secrets.md. This file exists only so the container can reach that
# secret — which is exactly what an ECS task definition carries, minus the
# credentials, because a task gets those from its role.

# --- Which secret to load -------------------------------------------------
# Unset it and the entrypoint does nothing, which is how you run against a
# fully hand-written environment.
WISHLY_SECRETS_ID=wishly/prod
AWS_REGION=us-east-1

# --- Credentials ----------------------------------------------------------
# Needed here and on the VM because neither is inside AWS and so neither can
# have a role. ECS sets none of these: the task role supplies temporary
# credentials through the container credentials endpoint, and boto3 finds them
# with no configuration.
#
# Scope this IAM user to exactly one action on one secret —
# secretsmanager:GetSecretValue on arn:...:secret:wishly/prod-*. It is a
# long-lived credential on a machine outside AWS; it should be worth as little
# as possible if it leaks.
AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# --- Cloudflare Tunnel ----------------------------------------------------
# The one value that cannot come from the secret: cloudflared is Cloudflare's
# image, not ours, so it has no entrypoint of ours to load anything. On ECS the
# task definition injects it into that container natively from the same secret.
TUNNEL_TOKEN=
